{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1792000000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5b.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-11 10:45:49 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-11 17:45:49 Asia/Bangkok\n\n# Adversarial review: slice 5b (A -> R, linear scan over A's liveness)\n\nReviewer: claude-fable-5-1 (Adversarial Reviewer / Lead System Architect). Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5b`, branch `srvm-phase5b`, HEAD b2eadc83, diff `git diff master...HEAD` (3 files: `derive.cljc` +68, `derive_test.cljc` +352, new `debruijn_register_code_test.cljc`). Nothing staged or committed by me. Source tree verified clean against HEAD before and after every mutant (`cmp` against my own verified backup `collab/p5b-fable-derive.verified-backup.cljc`, then `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` exit 0, then `git status --short` showing only `collab/`).\n\nI read the brief, the author report, design §0, §1-§2, §4, §5, §6.1-6.3, §8, §10.2, §12, `derive.cljc`, `analysis.cljc`, `code.cljc` (operand table, `rd`, `uses`, `tail-call?`), `debruijn_register_code.cljc` (opcode table, `body-liveness`, the twelve validator rules), the full `derive_test.cljc`, the author's mutation script/log, and the three slow-lane logs. I did not trust either report; everything below was re-derived or re-run here.\n\n## What I ran (all foreground, JVM)\n\n| Run | Command | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Baseline fast lane | `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test -n yin.vm.debruijn-register-code-test` | 22 tests, 6200 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors (6 s) |\n| 33 mutants | `python3 -I collab/p5b-fable-mutate.py` (each: exact-string edit, `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test`, restore from backup, byte compare) | table below; `cmp` RESTORED_OK after each batch |\n| Hash recompute + widening probe | a throwaway test ns copied to `test/p5b_fable/probe_test.clj`, run with `clojure -M:test -n p5b-fable.probe-test`, then deleted (`git status` clean; the sandbox refused every `clojure -A:test -M -e/-i` and `-Sdeps` form, so this was the only route) | hashes match; probe image recorded below |\n| kondo | `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the 3 paths | errors 0, warnings 0 |\n| Trailing whitespace | `grep -nE '[[:space:]]+$'` on the 3 paths | no matches |\n| cljstyle | `cljstyle check` on the 3 paths | **NOT RUN**: sandbox \"This command requires approval\" (same refusal the author hit) |\n| Slow C4 row, Node and Dart lanes | not run (per instructions). I verified the author's logs instead: `p5b-opus-jvm-slow.log` lines 50-53 \"C4 completed…\", \"Ran 1 tests containing 90 assertions. 0 failures, 0 errors\"; `p5b-opus-node-sub-cljs.log` lines 95, 191 (both `Testing` lines), 235 (C4 completed), 259-260 (1258 / 57700 / 0 / 0); `p5b-opus-dart-sub-cljd.log` line 1452 (C4 completed), 1458 \"+1187: All tests passed!\" | consistent with the report |\n\n## Findings (severity | file:line | evidence | correction)\n\n**F1. MEDIUM | test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:690 (`widened-layout-interval`) | the §2.2 widened-start rule has no test that can fail.** Mutant M06 (start = first syntactic definition, i.e. the un-widened frozen rule the design explicitly refines) SURVIVES the whole namespace. The test's program `(if c (resume :p 1) 2)` has no temporaries in the else arm, so widened and un-widened scans give the identical image (the design itself says \"nothing observable changes\" for that row). The rule *is* observable on `(if c (resume :p 1) (g 2))`: A `[[:var 1 c] [:branch-false 1 5] [:const 2 1] [:resume :p 2] [:jump 8] [:var 3 g] [:const 4 2] [:call 0 3 [4] false] [:halt 0]]`, `L(4)={0}` before `v0`'s only def at 7, so `v0:[4,7]` is assigned at pc 4 and takes T0 ahead of the else-arm temporaries. Implementation output (probe, hand-derivation agrees): `{:bodies [{:locals 0 :registers 3 :start 0 :end 8}] :instructions [[:load-free 0 c] [:branch-false 0 5] [:const 0 1] [:resume :p 0] [:jump 8] [:load-free 1 g] [:const 2 2] [:call 0 1 [2] false []] [:halt 0]]}`. Under M06 the same program gives `g→T0, 2→T1, v0→T2` (`[:call 2 0 [1] false []] [:halt 2]`), a different but equally valid image, so neither the validator nor the oracle can tell them apart; only a pinned golden can. The test name and comment (\"§6.3's numeric note: v0 is live at jump pc 4 before its sole def at 5\") claim coverage the assertion does not provide. The implementation is correct per §2.2; this is a test gap. **Correction:** pin the probe image above (or add it as a second case in `widened-layout-interval`); keep the existing case. Not blocking: the implementation follows the design and the un-widened alternative is also a sound allocation, so no runtime behaviour is at risk.\n\n**F2. LOW | derive_test.cljc:703 (`hand-derived-register-intervals`) | tests the design's numbers, not the implementation.** It recomputes intervals with its own closure over `analysis` and compares to pins; it never calls `register-image` or `allocation`. Fine as a §6.3 number check (that is what §10.2 item 1's \"verify every hand-derived number\" asks), but its placement after the implementation tests invites misreading it as interval coverage of the allocator. Correction: one-line docstring saying it checks §2.2's arithmetic, not the allocator (optional).\n\n**F3. LOW | derive_test.cljc:645-687 | progress `println`s in `c4-execution-oracle`.** Five print sites. Three other guarded slow namespaces also print, so there is precedent, and on Node/Dart they are the only evidence item 9 completed. Acceptable; the orchestrator may ask to trim to the single completion line.\n\n**F4. LOW (judgement on the author's finding 1) | `debruijn_register_code.cljc:269` | `register-hash` is SHA-256.** Pre-existing, untouched, and §2.1 says it is not changed in 5b. The brief's \"only SHA-256 use is the existing descriptor-hash function\" is loose wording for \"no new SHA-256 use\"; 5b adds none. No action in this slice; whether `register-hash` moves to BLAKE3 belongs to the cutover (§8.2) and the owner.\n\n**F5. INFO | derive.cljc:178 | the `sort-by (juxt end id)` on `expired` is behaviourally dead.** Expired slots are poured into a sorted set, so expiry order can never affect the result (mutant M32 survives for that reason). The design asks for the frozen order \"verbatim\", so leaving it is defensible; it costs nothing. No change requested.\n\nNo HIGH findings. No design departures found. Every pinned §6.3 R image I re-derived matched, every hand-derived number I checked matched, and no validator, oracle or scope rule is violated.\n\n## Answers\n\n### 1. Tests that can fail, per acceptance item, and the mutant table\n\nItem → test that fails on a plausible defect (from the table): 1 `register-goldens` (M01, M02, M05, M07-M13, M19, M22-M24, M26-M30, M33); 2 `register-images-validate` (M08-M14, M17, M18, M21, M24-M26); 3 `two-liveness-analyses-agree` (M01, M14, M15, M17-M25, M30); 4 `structural-slot` (M08, M09, M11, M19, M22, M24, M27, M29, M33); 5 `register-determinism` (fails only when the image throws: M04, M07, M12, M21; by construction it cannot see host-order dependence, see M03); 6 `register-execution-oracle` (M01, M04, M07-M10, M14, M17-M24, M26, M30); 7 `lexical-address-law` (M04, M07, M12, M21 via throws; on its own it checks the address legs, not allocation); 8 `worked-example-slots` (M01, M02, M04, M05, M07, M12-M14, M19, M22, M28-M30); 9 not mutated here (slow); 10 trivial pin; 11 kondo verified.\n\nMutant table (all against `derive.cljc`, `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test`, 21 tests; restore + `cmp` after each):\n\n| # | Mutant | Result | Failing deftests |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M01 | expiry `<` → `<=` | KILLED (21 fail) | execution-oracle, goldens, operand-goldens, two-liveness, worked-example |\n| M02 | lowest-free → highest-free (`first` → `last`) | KILLED (4) | goldens, worked-example-slots |\n| M03 | free set `(sorted-set)` → `#{}` | **SURVIVED** | — (see judgement) |\n| M04 | points = defs only (no L) | KILLED (71 fail, 7 err) | 8 tests |\n| M05 | points = L only (no `defs(p)`) | KILLED (13) | goldens, operand-goldens, worked-example |\n| M06 | start = first def only (no widening over layout L) | **SURVIVED** | — (F1) |\n| M07 | end = last def (not widened to last use) | KILLED (71 fail, 7 err) | 8 tests |\n| M08 | drop structural reservation (`(if missing 1 0)` → `0`) | KILLED (29) | execution-oracle, goldens, validate, structural-slot |\n| M09 | structural slot = locals+peak+1 | KILLED (18) | execution-oracle, goldens, validate, structural-slot |\n| M10 | `locals` = arity+1 | KILLED (83) | execution-oracle, goldens, validate |\n| M11 | physical register without `locals` offset | KILLED (91) | goldens, validate, structural-slot |\n| M12 | body `:end` exclusive | KILLED (101 fail, 112 err) | 10 tests |\n| M13 | body `:start` +1 | KILLED (339) | 6 tests |\n| M14 | `live` never filled | KILLED (50) | execution-oracle, goldens, validate, two-liveness, worked-example |\n| M15 | `live` from the allocator's own `L(p+1) − rd` (wrong analysis) | KILLED (3) | goldens (row 10 tail), two-liveness |\n| M16 | as M15 but tail calls forced `[]` | **SURVIVED** | — (see judgement) |\n| M17 | tail-call `live` non-empty (`[0]`) | KILLED (60) | execution-oracle, goldens, validate, two-liveness |\n| M18 | `live` reversed (not ascending) | KILLED (21) | execution-oracle, validate, two-liveness |\n| M19 | all `:regs` operands reversed (`:call`/`:ffi-call` args) | KILLED (141) | 6 tests |\n| M20 | `:ffi-call` args reversed only | KILLED (9) | execution-oracle (`:ffi-two-operands`), operand-goldens, two-liveness |\n| M21 | `:stream-put` stream/value swapped | KILLED (3 fail, 7 err) | execution-oracle (`:put-on-made-stream`), operand-goldens, validate, … |\n| M22 | `:call` rd/operator swapped | KILLED (305) | 5 tests |\n| M23 | `:define` rd := rs | KILLED (10) | execution-oracle, goldens, two-liveness |\n| M24 | `:resume` value-reg +1 | KILLED (24) | 6 tests incl. structural-slot, widened-layout-interval |\n| M25 | `:park`/`:current-continuation` rd +1 | KILLED (18) | validate, two-liveness |\n| M26 | closure arity −1 (in `resolve`) | KILLED (166) | register + stack goldens/validate/oracles, resolve test |\n| M27 | definition-less id → `locals+0` instead of structural | KILLED (4) | goldens, structural-slot |\n| M28 | `active` filter `>=` → `>` (expired dropped without freeing) | KILLED (9) | goldens, widened-layout-interval, worked-example |\n| M29 | `next-slot` always advances | KILLED (10) | goldens, structural-slot, widened-layout-interval, worked-example |\n| M30 | taken slot not removed from free set | KILLED (9) | execution-oracle, goldens, two-liveness, worked-example |\n| M31 | assignment order at one pc: decreasing id | **SURVIVED** | — (unobservable by construction) |\n| M32 | expiry `sort-by` removed | **SURVIVED** | — (unobservable by construction, F5) |\n| M33 | structural slot aliases the last temporary | KILLED (4) | goldens, structural-slot |\n\n28 killed, 5 survived. Judgement on the survivors:\n- **M06 is the one real gap (F1).** Killable with one golden.\n- M03: on the JVM a `PersistentHashSet` of small ints iterates in ascending order, so `#{}` is indistinguishable from `sorted-set` here; `register-determinism` compares two runs in one process and cannot see host-order dependence by construction. The implementation uses `sorted-set` as §12 item 1 requires; the three-host green lanes are the only evidence that order never enters. Acceptable.\n- M16: §2.5 states the two analyses agree on every sound allocation and proves why (forward edges, injective slot map over simultaneously live ids). A reading of the design that fills `live` from `L(p+1)` with a tail special case therefore produces identical images on every program and no test can tell; the design explicitly makes `body-liveness` the production source as a format rule, not an observable. Survival is expected and benign.\n- M31/M32: at most one interval starts per pc on a well-formed vector (§2.3) and expiry order cannot matter with a sorted free set. Both orders are design verbatim, not behaviour.\n\n**The author's table.** Their ten mutants correspond to my M01, M02, M04, M10-ish (locals forced 0), M08, M14, M20, M21, M12, and a \"never free\" variant; all killed, matching my results. Their \"one survivor\" is in fact their mutant 9 (exclusive `:end`), which they classify as killed by an **error** (an index exception inside `body-liveness`), not an assertion. My M12 confirms: 112 errors **and 101 assertion failures** (`register-images-validate`'s descriptor and terminator checks, `widened-layout-interval`, the goldens on bodies that do not throw). So that mutant is killed by assertions as well, not only by the exception; no issue. Their mutant 7 (`:ffi-call` reversal) was expressed with an `(if (= op :ffi-call) …)` wrapper exactly as my M20, since the generic `:regs` path has no ffi-specific code; that is fine.\n\n### 2. Independent derivations of §6.3 R goldens\n\nDerived from §2.2-2.5 by hand before reading the pins; then compared.\n\n- **Row 2 `(f (g x) y)`.** `L(6)={0} L(5)={1,2,5} L(4)={1,2} L(3)={1,3,4} L(2)={1,3} L(1)={1}`. Intervals `v1:[0,5] v3:[1,3] v4:[2,3] v2:[3,5] v5:[4,5] v0:[5,6]`. Scan: T0,T1,T2; pc 3 `v2→T3`; pc 4 `v3,v4` expire (3<4), free {1,2}, `v5→T1`; pc 5 `v0→T2`. R: `[[:load-free 0 f] [:load-free 1 g] [:load-free 2 x] [:call 3 1 [2] false [0]] [:load-free 1 y] [:call 2 0 [3 1] false []] [:halt 2]]`, registers 4. `live` at 3 by `body-liveness`: live-in(4) = {0,3} (use ∅, out {0,3,1} minus def 1), live-out(3)={0,3} minus rd 3 = `[0]`. **Matches pin and `[:call 3 1 [2] false [0]]`.**\n- **Row 3 `((f))`.** `v2:[0,1] v1:[1,2] v0:[2,3]`; pc 2 `v2` expires → `v0→T0`. R `[[:load-free 0 f] [:call 1 0 [] false []] [:call 0 1 [] false []] [:halt 0]]`, registers 2. **Matches.**\n- **Row 5 `(f (if c 1 2) y)`.** `L(2)={1,3} L(3)={1} L(4)={1,2} L(5)={1} L(6)={1,2} L(7)={1,2,4} L(8)={0}`; `v1:[0,7] v3:[1,2] v2:[3,7] v4:[6,7] v0:[7,8]`; pc 3 `v3` expires → `v2→T1`; pc 6 `v4→T2`; pc 7 `v0→T3`. R `[… [:branch-false 1 5] [:const 1 1] [:jump 6] [:const 1 2] [:load-free 2 y] [:call 3 0 [1 2] false []] [:halt 3]]`, registers 4, live at 7 `{3}−3=[]`. **Matches.** The conditional's two arm definitions share T1 by construction (one virtual id, one slot).\n- **Row 6 body (tail call), `locals 1`.** `L(7)={1,2,3}` (no successor), `L(8)={0}`; `v1:[4,7] v2:[5,7] v3:[6,7] v0:[7,8]`; nothing expires at 7 (7<7 false) → `v0→T3`; peak 4, registers 5; physical = 1+slot. R `[[:load-free 1 +] [:load-bound 2 0 0] [:const 3 1] [:call 4 1 [2 3] true []] [:return 4]]`; main `[[:closure 0 1 4] [:const 1 10] [:call 2 0 [1] false []] [:halt 2]]` registers 3. **Matches, `live []` on the tail call.**\n- **Row 8 `(f (resume :p x) y)`.** `L(0)=L(1)={} L(2)={3} L(3)={1,2} L(4)={1,2,4} L(5)={0}`; `v1:[0,4] v3:[1,2] v4:[3,4] v0:[4,5]`, `v2` definition-less. pc 3 `v3` expires → `v4→T1`; pc 4 `v0→T2`; peak 3, structural T3, registers 4. R `[[:load-free 0 f] [:load-free 1 x] [:resume :p 1] [:load-free 1 y] [:call 2 0 [3 1] false []] [:halt 2]]`. **Matches.**\n- **Row 9 (inner call tail).** `L(3)={3,4}` (no successor) `L(4)={1,2} L(5)={1,2,5}`; `v1:[0,5] v3:[1,3] v4:[2,3] v2:[3,5] v5:[4,5] v0:[5,6]`; same slots as row 2; `live` at 3 is `[]` (no successor), at 5 `[]`. **Matches; the pinned `[:call 3 1 [2] true []]` is right.**\n- **Widened note `(if c (resume :p 1) 2)`.** `v1:[0,1] v2:[2,3] v0:[4,6]`; all take T0 in turn; registers 1. **Matches the pin in `widened-layout-interval`.**\n\nHashes recomputed through the public `rcode/register-hash` on the pinned literal images: row 2 `aaa109a179b7e4b280ef4c25006316067569bfaddb300d2b46ddadf9398d93fa`, row 6 `71926b1e251a07f8d1234c33e55c77f00fd387b24560a1a7578b95cbe510772e`, row 8 `52c71ac04f193913c33782d81ec5a2276db53753010f9888c53650f4252c134e`. All three equal the pinned strings (indices 1, 5, 8 of `register-golden-hashes`).\n\n### 3. Item 3, item 4, determinism, the oracle, the address law\n\n- **Two-liveness agreement (item 3):** `two-liveness-analyses-agree` iterates `C` plus row 9, every boundary pc, virtual live-out as the union of `analysis/live` over **`analysis/successors`** (actual successors; a tail `:call` gives none), minus `rd`, mapped through a slot map rebuilt from paired A/R operands (not the allocator's state; it also asserts the map is consistent on re-encounter), compared to the filled `live`; tail calls asserted `[] = []` on both sides. Independent of `body-liveness`. M14, M15, M17, M18 confirm it fails when either side is wrong.\n- **Structural slot (item 4):** for the three named programs: `registers = locals + peak + 1` with `peak` recomputed from the max assigned slot of defined ids; every definition-less id maps to `registers − 1`; the structural register appears as an operand only at pcs outside `analysis/reachable`; absent from every `live` at a reachable boundary. `missing` is computed from operands minus syntactic defs, so a mutant cannot vacate the assertions. M08, M09, M27, M33 die here.\n- **Determinism (item 5):** structures, encoded bytes, hashes, plus the three omitted-fact pairs on `:vector`/`:address`/R/hash. Correct per §6.2 items 5-6; see M03 for its inherent limit.\n- **Execution oracle (item 6):** counts 26/12/19/1 asserted; `:old` pipeline is `rc/adapt` over `vm/ast->datoms`, `:new` is `derive/register-image` over the projection, both into `rvm/create-vm` with the same composition (`list`/`vector`/`inc` added); `native-normalize` on both sides and on pinned expectations; `:op/ping` bridge; the two 5a-lesson rows (`:op/echo` two operands, `:stream/put` on a made stream) reach values on both pipelines and killed M20/M21. The old lowerers appear only in `register-run`, `stack-run` and `c4-backends`, i.e. as oracle inputs.\n- **Lexical-address law (item 7):** five legs (A, resolve(A), R, H, old resolver) over all of `C`. The §5.2 non-vacuity assertions live in the 5a sibling `lexical-address-law-stack` in the same namespace, not repeated here; acceptable.\n\n### 4. Scope and portability\n\n- Diff touches exactly the three §10.2 paths. `debruijn_register_code.cljc`, the kernel, the design: untouched.\n- `derive.cljc` requires only `code`, `db` (resolve-name), `analysis`, `rcode`; no old lowerer or kernel. No `#'` anywhere in the three files. No `for` in the slice (`doseq`/`mapv`/`reduce`/`into` only).\n- `sorted-set` is used for the free set; Dart lane green per the author's log. Slot maps start from `{}`; `(fnil conj [])` points; integer arithmetic only; catch clauses use `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`; pinned images are Clojure data in `.cljc`, no EDN strings. `(apply min/max points)` can never see an empty seq because every intervaled id has at least its def pc.\n- Emission reads `rcode/opcode-table` positionally and never `nth`s the `live` slot of the resolved tuple (it returns `[]` first), so the 4-operand A `:call` against the 5-operand R table is safe.\n\n### 5. Honest claims and the lane departure\n\n- Test names: `two-liveness-analyses-agree`, `register-determinism`, `lexical-address-law` are corpus claims, consistent with the design's \"property the gate checks on every corpus program\". `widened-layout-interval` overclaims (F1). `hand-derived-register-intervals` is a design-number check (F2).\n- Nothing in the implementation is resolved by reading the design differently from me; `:end` inclusive, expiry `end < p`, lowest free, structural slot `locals + peak`, layout A's, `live` from `body-liveness` all match §2 as I read it. M16 is the one alternative reading and it is image-equivalent.\n- The author's finding 1 (SHA-256) judged in F4: no 5b action.\n- **Background slow lanes:** the report is candid that the JVM slow row and the two subsystem lanes ran as harness background tasks (no `&`), and the three logs carry the completion line and the final counts (quoted in my table above). The purpose of \"foreground only\" is that a delegate's lanes die with its turn; these did not, and the counts are present. Acceptable for this report. Remaining lane gaps: cljstyle (neither the author nor I could run it in-sandbox) and the literal `bb test:sub:clj yin.vm`; the orchestrator should run both once before merging.\n\n## Verdict rationale\n\nThe implementation is a faithful §2 lowering: every re-derived golden and hash matches, 28/33 adversarial mutants die on named tests, the oracle and the two-liveness gate are independent and do fail, scope and portability are clean. The single real gap is a test that cannot fail on the widened-start rule (F1), which is a one-golden addition with no implementation change and no runtime risk, so I do not withhold merge for it; the orchestrator may fold F1 into the merge or a follow-up at their discretion.\n\nVERDICT: READY_TO_MERGE\n# yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations: phase 5, the de Bruijn images and the scanners lowered from A\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 5 of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\n§10.** Rewritten 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) under the\nowner's rulings of that date (§0.1), from the frozen design as amended\n(`ab952ace`), the landed phase-3 code\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/`), the de Bruijn image formats,\nvalidators and kernels as they stand, the linker and dependency-closure\ncode, and the corpora the gates run on. Earlier revisions of this\ndocument (route (b), the adapter over the old lowerers, codex rounds\n1-3) are superseded; what they settled about the scanners, the C4 gate,\npublic access and portability is carried over unchanged. The frozen\ndocument is not changed by this one: §9 records findings with exact\namendment text. Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md),\nincluding its unitarity invariant (§0.1 item 4), and to\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md)\n(\"the frozen design\"); the format and rigor model is\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.evaluator.md).\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (follows from an owner\nruling, frozen text, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design\nchoice of this document, open to the reviewer). File references are to\nthe worktree at `ab952ace`; line numbers are those of that commit.\n\nContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the resolved\nvector · §2 A → R, linear-scan allocation over A's liveness · §3 A → H,\nstackification over A's expression structure · §4 determinism and the\nfacts A omits · §5 the lexical-address law · §6 verification: the\nexecution oracle, validators, goldens · §7 the scanners and the linker\nrecords · §8 regeneration and what keeps the old lowerers alive · §9\nfindings and amendments (frozen design, linker, UCF) · §10 slices and\ngates · §11 owner questions · §12 portability.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n\n### 0.1 Owner rulings of 2026-10-11 (DECIDED, binding)\n\n1. **R and H are lowered from A.** A, the semantic tuples, is the\n   linearization of the AST; lowering from the tree would re-linearize.\n   One linearization, everything else derives from it: same A, same R,\n   same H.\n2. **No legacy.** No version bump (`\"b2\"`/`\"r2\"` keep their names; no\n   `b3`/`r3`), no re-version procedure, no lift-contract question, no\n   profile-string question. The lowerers change in place. Pinned\n   checksums, goldens and any descriptor hashing a lowerer's contract are\n   regenerated once (§8 says which, and how a reviewer verifies the\n   regeneration is mechanical).\n3. **No adapter over the old lowerers.** New derivations: A → R as\n   linear-scan register allocation over A's liveness (frozen §8.2 is the\n   starting point), A → H as stackification over A's expression\n   structure (frozen §8.3); both pure functions of A. `lower-register`,\n   `lower-stack` and `debruijn-resolve` are deleted at cutover (phase 8);\n   §8.3 says what keeps them alive until then.\n4. **Unitarity.** `datom.world.md` (branch `worktree-invariant-unitarity`,\n   invariant list): \"Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are\n   append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The\n   Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is\n   lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form\n   (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it\n   and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\" No design may assume\n   a receiver without a tree. The scanners and provenance may read the\n   tree; the derivations of R and H do not need it. §9.2 names the\n   conflicting statements in the linker and UCF designs and proposes\n   amendment text.\n5. **Verification replaces the byte oracle.** The old compilers are an\n   execution oracle only: the corpus (B0, the register corpus, the C4\n   linked-prelude corpus by name, read-only) runs through old and new\n   pipelines and must give identical observable results under a stated\n   normalization. Validators and liveness checks run on every output;\n   hand-derived goldens pin the tricky cases; a determinism check covers\n   same-A-same-bytes and ASTs differing only in facts A omits.\n\n### 0.2 The answer\n\nPhase 5 is three pure functions of an admitted register-shaped vector\n`v` (A = `ucf/code-address v`). `resolve` (§1) rewrites every `:var` to\n`:load-bound`/`:load-free` and every `:closure` to its arity, using the\nclosure-owner tree the validator already established; its output is the\n*resolved vector*, same pcs, same virtual registers, a stage value with\nno identity. `register-image` (§2) allocates physical temporaries per\nbody by a linear scan over intervals computed from the phase-3 liveness\n`L`, fills the `live` operands with the format's one liveness function,\nand emits the existing `:yin.debruijn.register/*` image; the allocation\nrule is the frozen §8.2's, closed where that text left choices open and\ncorrected in one place (§9.1 F8). `stack-image` (§3) emits the existing\n`:yin.debruijn.code/*` image by one walk over the parse trees the\nvalidator recovers: operator, push, operands with pushes, call, labels\nfor conditionals, bodies out of line in FIFO order. Neither function\nreads the tree, a side table, or anything but `v`; neither calls an old\nlowerer. The image formats, descriptors, validators and kernels are\nunchanged, so every existing refusal rule, the `live-exact` recomputation\nand the kernels' execution semantics still admit and run the new images.\nThe hand-derived goldens of §6.3 fix the bytes for `(+ 1 2)`,\n`(f (g x) y)`, `((f))`, two conditionals, a tail call, two definitions and\na terminal operand; the execution oracle of §6.1 runs every corpus\nprogram through the old and new pipelines on the same kernels and\nrequires identical observable results; §4 shows, with evidence, that\nthe facts A omits cannot reach R or H. The scanners (§7) are unchanged\nfrom the reviewed design.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The resolved vector\n\n### 1.1 Where it lives, what it takes, what it returns\n\n**PROPOSED.** Namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.derive`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc`, beside the landed\nphase-3 namespaces. It requires `yin.vm.semantic-register.code`\n(`parse`, `body-ranges`, `body-index`, `rd`, `uses`, `tail-call?`),\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis` (`analyze`, `live`),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name` only), `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code`\n(`body-liveness`, `live-slot-index`, `boundary-opcodes`, the image\nshape it validates) and `yin.vm.debruijn-code` (nothing but the opcode\ntable it emits into; no function). It does **not** require\n`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`, `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`,\n`yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile`, `yin.vm.linearize`, `yin.vm.code`,\nor either kernel.\n\n```clojure\n(resolve v)   ; v: a vector code/well-formed? accepted\n;; => {:vector rv            ; the resolved vector: same length, same pcs, same ids\n;;     :bodies bodies        ; code/parse's bodies with :expr trees, :owner, :start, :end\n;;     :names  {pc name}}    ; diagnostic: the name every :load-bound replaced\n```\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §8.2 step 1: \"the output is a stage value with no\nidentity\"): the resolved vector is never hashed, never persisted, never\nserved, never a fetch key. The namespace exports no digest of it.\n**DECIDED** (precondition): `v` has passed `code/well-formed?`\n(`code.cljc:842-853`); `resolve` re-runs `code/parse` (`:856-861`)\nbecause `load-vector` strips the `:expr` trees (`:879`), the same stance\nfrozen §10 phase 3 takes for `:analysis`.\n\n### 1.2 The closure-owner tree and the lexical chain\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §3.4 rule 6; `code/body-ranges`, `code.cljc:256-276`;\n`partition-defect`, `:292-336`). `body-ranges` gives, in pc order, one\nbody per distinct `:closure` body-pc plus main, each with `:owner`, the\npc of the `:closure` naming it (nil for main); `partition-defect` has\nestablished that every non-main body has exactly one owner in another\nbody and that following owners reaches main.\n\n```\nchain(main)   = []\nchain(body i) = (cons params(owner_i) (chain (body-of owner_i)))\nparams(c)     = (nth (nth v c) 2)                 ; [:closure rd params body-pc]\nbody-of(pc)   = (code/body-index bodies pc)\n```\n\n`chain` is innermost-first, the `stack` argument `debruijn/resolve-name`\ntakes (`debruijn.cljc:617-637`).\n\n### 1.3 The rewrite\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §8.2 step 1). For every pc of `v`:\n\n| A tuple | resolved tuple |\n|---|---|\n| `[:var rd name]`, `(resolve-name (chain (body-of pc)) name)` = `{:bound [d q]}` | `[:load-bound rd d q]` |\n| `[:var rd name]`, `{:free name}` | `[:load-free rd name]` |\n| `[:closure rd params body-pc]` | `[:closure rd (count params) body-pc]` |\n| every other tuple | unchanged |\n\nDuplicate parameters and shadowing are `resolve-name`'s by construction:\nrightmost wins within a frame (`debruijn.cljc:621-624`; pinned by\n`debruijn_test.cljc:391-402`), innermost frame wins across frames\n(`:363-389`), free names preserved exactly (`:405-410`). A's\n`:closure params` is the front end's vector verbatim (item 2 `:syms`,\n`code.cljc:177`; `linearize.cljc:105`), so the frames searched are the\nbinders the program wrote. Rule R needs no check here: item 11\n(`code.cljc:663-675`) has refused any `:var` naming `yin/def`, so no\n`:load-free yin/def` can be emitted; the kernels' `reserved-rule`\n(`debruijn_register_code.cljc:663-675`, `debruijn_code.cljc:864`) would\nrefuse it anyway.\n\n### 1.4 Parse trees and layout, carried as derived data\n\nThe resolved vector keeps A's pcs, so `code/parse`'s trees\n(`{:op :pc :children}`, conditionals `{:op :if :pc :jump :end :children\n[test then else]}`, `code.cljc:350-406`) and `analysis/analyze`'s\n`:live`, `:successors` (`analysis.cljc:70-83`) index it directly. §2 and\n§3 consume `{:vector :bodies}` plus `(analysis/analyze v)`; nothing\nelse.\n\n---\n\n## 2. A → R: linear-scan allocation over A's liveness\n\n### 2.1 The output format, unchanged\n\n**DECIDED.** The image is `{:bodies [{:locals n :registers k :start s :end e} …]\n:instructions [tuple …]}` over `rcode/opcode-table`\n(`debruijn_register_code.cljc:68-149`), `:end` **inclusive**\n(`:243-245`, `body-liveness` `(range start (inc end))`, `:352`); body 0\nis main with `:locals 0`; a lambda body's `:locals` is its arity and\nregisters `0..locals-1` are the L bank, never written or read by any\ninstruction (`:17-27`: `:load-bound` reads the frame chain into a fresh\ntemporary); a temporary `Tᵢ` is physical register `locals + i`. The\ndescriptor (`:190-204`), `contract-version` 4 (`:52-59`), the encoder\n(`:221-254`), `register-hash` (`:261-269`) and the twelve validator rules\n(`:698-705`) are **not** changed. `contract-version`'s docstring sentence\n\"bumped whenever this dimension's … allocation … change shape\" is\nsuperseded by ruling 2 and is amended at cutover (§8.2); the number is\nnot bumped.\n\n### 2.2 Intervals\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §8.2 (\"Intervals\"), **refined** (§9.1 F8) so that\nevery live point is covered. For one body with pcs `[s, e)` (`e`\nexclusive as `code/body-ranges` gives it), `L(p) = (analysis/live a p)`\n(`analysis.cljc:86-89`), `defs(v)` the pcs whose `rd` slot names `v`\n(`code/rd`), the virtual ids with at least one syntactic definition are\n**intervaled**:\n\n```\npoints(v) = defs(v) ∪ {p ∈ [s,e) : v ∈ L(p)}\nstart(v)  = (apply min points(v))\nend(v)    = (apply max points(v))\n```\n\nOn reachable code `start(v)` is the first syntactic definition (definite\nassignment, frozen §3.4 item 8, puts every read after a write, and\n`L` points before the first definition arise only in layout syntax: see\nthe `(if c (resume :p 1) 2)` note in §6.3 row 7). The frozen text's\n\"start = first syntactic definition\" is therefore kept where it matters\nand widened only over layout syntax, where an uncovered live point would\notherwise let the scan assign a slot the layout instruction still names.\nA conditional's `rd` has one interval spanning both arms from its first\narm definition to its last use, as frozen §8.2 states. A tail `:call`'s\n`rd` and the `:return r` that names it form an interval `[call-pc,\nreturn-pc]` of a slot never written at runtime.\n\n**Definition-less ids** (frozen §3.4 item 7: an id whose every leaf is a\n`:resume`) have no interval and no slot; where layout syntax names one\n(a wholly terminal body's `[:return r]`/`[:halt r]`, an enclosing\ninstruction's operand after a terminal child) the operand lowers to the\n**structural slot** `T_k`, `k` the body's peak count (§2.3). It is never\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '240,295p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md; sed -n '675,750p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc; sed -n '125,190p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short; git rev-parse --short HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '240,295p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md; sed -n '675,750p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc; sed -n '125,190p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short; git rev-parse --short HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"**structural slot** `T_k`, `k` the body's peak count (§2.3). It is never\nwritten, never read at runtime, never in a saved window; it may appear in\nthe static `live` operand of an unreachable boundary instruction, a\nstatic fact of the format and nothing more (frozen §8.2 \"Definition-less\nids\", retained).\n\n### 2.3 The scan\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §8.2 \"Scan\", with its two open choices closed):\n\n```\nfree   := the empty sorted set of temporary indices\nnext   := 0                                   ; next never-used index\npeak   := 0\nslot   := {}                                  ; virtual id → temporary index\nfor p from s to e-1:\n  for each intervaled v, in increasing end(v) then increasing v,\n      with end(v) < p and v not yet expired:        free := free ∪ {slot(v)}\n  for each intervaled v with start(v) = p, in increasing v:\n     if free is non-empty: i := (first free); free := free − {i}\n     else:                 i := next; next := next + 1       ; a never-used slot\n     slot(v) := i; peak := max(peak, i+1)\nregisters := locals + peak + (1 if the body names a definition-less id, else 0)\n```\n\n- The two loops are the frozen scan's, with their **total order**\n  (increasing `end` then increasing virtual id for expiry; increasing\n  virtual id for assignment) kept verbatim, so the result never depends\n  on how many intervals happen to start or expire at one pc. On a\n  well-formed vector at most one interval starts per pc (one instruction\n  defines one register; a layout-syntax live point belongs to one\n  enclosing destination), but the implementation does **not** rely on\n  that: the loop runs over whatever starts. `next` advances only when a\n  never-used slot is taken, so `peak = next` at the end of the scan.\n- **No same-instruction source/destination reuse** (frozen): expiry is\n  `end < p`, so a source whose interval ends at `p` is still assigned\n  when `p`'s destination is allocated; the destination never takes a\n  source's slot.\n- **Lowest free index first**; `next` only when the free set is empty.\n- Physical register of `v` is `locals + slot(v)`; the structural slot is\n  `locals + peak`.\n- Reservation (an interval holding a slot across a conditional's arms)\n  decides allocation only; liveness (§2.5) comes from the format's own\n  function, never from the scan (frozen §8.2 \"Reservation vs liveness\").\n\n### 2.4 Emission\n\nEach tuple of the resolved vector at pc `p` maps to one register tuple\nat the same pc, every `:reg`/`:regs` operand replaced by its physical\nregister, every literal operand copied, and the boundary opcodes\n(`rcode/boundary-opcodes`, `:156-159`) given a placeholder `live`\n`[]` that §2.5 fills:\n\n| resolved tuple | register tuple |\n|---|---|\n| `[:const rd v]` | `[:const R(rd) v]` |\n                                  [(vm/halted? task) (vm/blocked? task) (render/output (vm/value task))]))\n                      old (observe :old) new (observe :new)]\n                  (is (= old new))\n                  (is (= [true false {:py/out output :py/exception nil}] old new)))))))\n        (doseq [[label ast] modules family [:stack :register]]\n          (println \"C4 module\" label family)\n          (testing (str \"module \" label \" \" family)\n            (let [old (c4-task family :old ast source) new (c4-task family :new ast source)\n                  observe (fn [task] [(vm/halted? task) (vm/blocked? task) (native-normalize (vm/store task))])]\n              (is (= (observe old) (observe new)))\n              (is (= [true false] [(vm/halted? new) (vm/blocked? new)]))\n              (is (= (prelude/defined-keys ast) (set (keys (vm/store old))) (set (keys (vm/store new))))))))\n        (println \"C4 completed: 10 root programs; 8 module bodies; both kernel families\")\n        ;; Install-child execution over newly published images is phase 8.\n        ))))\n\n\n(deftest widened-layout-interval\n  ;; §6.3's numeric note: v0 is live at jump pc 4 before its sole def at 5.\n  (let [a (projected (if-node (v 'c) (resume :p (lit 1)) (lit 2)))\n        image (derive/register-image a)]\n    (is (= '{:bodies [{:locals 0 :registers 1 :start 0 :end 6}]\n             :instructions [[:load-free 0 c] [:branch-false 0 5] [:const 0 1]\n                            [:resume :p 0] [:jump 6] [:const 0 2] [:halt 0]]}\n           image))))\n\n\n(deftest hand-derived-register-intervals\n  (let [pins [{0 [3 4] 1 [0 3] 2 [1 3] 3 [2 3]}\n              {0 [5 6] 1 [0 5] 2 [3 5] 3 [1 3] 4 [2 3] 5 [4 5]}\n              {0 [2 3] 1 [1 2] 2 [0 1]}\n              {0 [2 5] 1 [0 1]}\n              {0 [7 8] 1 [0 7] 2 [3 7] 3 [1 2] 4 [6 7]}\n              {0 [2 3] 1 [0 2] 2 [1 2]}\n              {0 [1 2] 1 [0 1]}\n              {0 [4 5] 1 [3 4] 2 [0 3] 3 [1 3] 4 [2 3]}\n              {0 [4 5] 1 [0 4] 3 [1 2] 4 [3 4]}\n              {0 [5 6] 1 [0 5] 2 [3 5] 3 [1 3] 4 [2 3] 5 [4 5]}]]\n    (doseq [[i ast expected] (map vector (range) golden-asts pins)]\n      (let [a (projected ast) facts (analysis/analyze a)\n            interval (fn [start end]\n                       (let [defined (into #{} (keep #(code/rd (nth a %))) (range start end))]\n                         (into {} (map (fn [r]\n                                         (let [points (keep (fn [pc]\n                                                              (when (or (= r (code/rd (nth a pc)))\n                                                                        (contains? (analysis/live facts pc) r)) pc))\n                                                            (range start end))]\n                                           [r [(apply min points) (apply max points)]]))) defined)))\n            end (:end (first (code/body-ranges a)))]\n        (is (= expected (interval 0 end)) (str \"§6.3 image \" (inc i)))\n        (when (= i 5)\n          (is (= {0 [7 8] 1 [4 7] 2 [5 7] 3 [6 7]} (interval 4 9))))))))\n                             (vswap! queue conj [l (get by-start y)])\n                             (diagnostic! {:kind :closure :params (nth (nth v pc) 2) :source pc})\n                             (emit! [:closure x l]))\n                  :var (do (diagnostic! {:kind :var :source pc})\n                           (emit! (if (= op :load-bound) [:load-bound x y] [:load-free x])))\n                  :call (do (doseq [child children] (walk child) (emit! [:push]))\n                            (emit! [:call (count y) z]))\n                  :ffi-call (do (doseq [child children] (walk child) (emit! [:push]))\n                                (emit! [:ffi-call x (count y)]))\n                  :stream-put (do (walk (nth children 0)) (emit! [:push])\n                                  (walk (nth children 1)) (emit! [:stream-put]))\n                  (:define :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :resume)\n                  (do (walk (first children))\n                      (emit! (case op\n                               :define [:define x]\n                               :resume [:resume (nth (nth rv pc) 1)]\n                               [op])))\n                  (:const :gensym :store-get :stream-make) (emit! [op x])\n                  :store-put (emit! [op x y])\n                  (:current-continuation :park) (emit! [op]))))\n            (body!\n              [{:keys [expr owner]}]\n              (walk expr)\n              (emit! [(if (nil? owner) :halt :return)]))]\n      (body! (first bodies))\n      (loop [i 0]\n        (when-let [[l body] (get @queue i)]\n          (mark! l) (body! body) (recur (inc i))))\n      {:image (mapv (fn [t]\n                      (case (first t)\n                        (:jump :branch-false) (assoc t 1 (get @labels (nth t 1)))\n                        :closure (assoc t 2 (get @labels (nth t 2)))\n                        t)) @image)\n       :side-table @side})))\n\n\n(defn- allocation\n  [v a {:keys [start end owner]}]\n  (let [locals (if (nil? owner) 0 (count (nth (nth v owner) 2)))\n        defined (into #{} (keep #(code/rd (nth v %))) (range start end))\n        points (reduce (fn [m pc]\n                         (reduce (fn [m r] (update m r (fnil conj []) pc)) m\n                                 (into (analysis/live a pc) (analysis/defs v pc))))\n                       {} (range start end))\n        intervals (mapv (fn [r] [r (apply min (get points r)) (apply max (get points r))])\n                        (sort defined))\n        starts-by-pc (group-by second intervals)\n        missing (seq (remove defined (mapcat #(code/uses (nth v %)) (range start end))))]\n    (loop [pc start active [] free (sorted-set) next-slot 0 slots {}]\n      (if (= pc end)\n        {:locals locals :registers (+ locals next-slot (if missing 1 0))\n         :start start :end (dec end)\n         :slots slots :structural (+ locals next-slot)}\n        (let [expired (sort-by (juxt #(nth % 2) first)\n                               (filter #(< (nth % 2) pc) active))\n              free (into free (map #(get slots (first %))) expired)\n              active (filterv #(>= (nth % 2) pc) active)\n              starts (get starts-by-pc pc [])\n              [free next-slot slots]\n              (reduce (fn [[free next-slot slots] [r]]\n                        (let [i (if (seq free) (first free) next-slot)]\n                          [(disj free i) (if (seq free) next-slot (inc next-slot))\n                           (assoc slots r i)]))\n                      [free next-slot slots] starts)]\n          (recur (inc pc) (into active starts) free next-slot slots))))))\n\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time repo-wide.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time repo-wide to avoid contention.\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`).\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n?? collab/1791950000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b.cmd.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791950000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791950000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b.opus.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791950000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791950000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791950000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b.report.md\n?? collab/1792000000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5b.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n?? collab/1792000000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5b.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792000000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5b.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792020000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792020000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5b-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/p5b-c4-timing.clj\n?? collab/p5b-dart.log\n?? collab/p5b-derive.verified-backup.cljc\n?? collab/p5b-descriptor-g0.log\n?? collab/p5b-fable-derive.verified-backup.cljc\n?? collab/p5b-fable-hash.clj\n?? collab/p5b-fable-mutate.py\n?? collab/p5b-fable-probe/deps.edn\n?? collab/p5b-fable-probe/p5b_fable/probe_test.clj\n?? collab/p5b-g0-final.log\n?? collab/p5b-jvm-c4-thread-dump.log\n?? collab/p5b-jvm-fast.log\n?? collab/p5b-jvm-final.log\n?? collab/p5b-jvm-slow.log\n?? collab/p5b-mutations.clj\n?? collab/p5b-mutations.log\n?? collab/p5b-node-targeted.log\n?? collab/p5b-node.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-dart-slow.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-dart-sub-cljd.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-dart.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-derive.verified-backup.cljc\n?? collab/p5b-opus-jvm-fast.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-jvm-slow.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-node-slow.log\n?? collab/p5b-opus-node-sub-cljs.log\n?? c